--- field object.certificate.digest
type: bin

The SHA-256 digest of the certificate's DER encoding: its fingerprint, the
name a distrust gives it and the one `trust list` prints, carried as 32
bytes rather than as hexadecimal. The name to search by, because it is the
only one that identifies a certificate: two certificates can share a
subject name, and a subject name can be forged.

--- field object.certificate.name
type: str

The certificate's subject distinguished name, in the form `trust show`
prints it, such as `CN=DigiCert TLS ECC P384 Root G5,O=DigiCert\, Inc.,C=US`.
For a person to read; it does not identify the certificate on its own.
Search by `object.certificate.digest`.

--- field object.certificate.purposes
type: str.enum[]
values: server-auth | code-signing
closed: false

What the certificate is trusted for, one value per purpose, each once. The
registry spells a purpose in the form `ServerAuth`; the record carries it in
kebab-case, a hyphen at each change from lower to upper case and in place
of anything that is not a letter or a digit, so `ServerAuth` is
`server-auth` and `CodeSigning` is `code-signing`. A root from the shipped
bundle is trusted for `server-auth`. An administrator may record any other
purpose, and it appears here in the same form.

--- event trustd.root.added
tier: standard
gating: none — every root that enters the set in force is recorded
cardinality: one per root that entered the set

A root certificate authority entered the set this machine trusts. trustd
recomposes the set whenever the registry's additions or distrusts change,
or the shipped bundle is replaced, and writes one record for each root that
is in the new set and was not in the one before: an addition, a distrust
lifted, or a root a bundle upgrade brought in.

The set trustd composes when it starts is the baseline these records count
from, and is not recorded. A change made while trustd was not running is
seen only as part of that baseline.

No subject. Who wrote an addition or lifted a distrust is the registry's to
record (LCS, where the key is audited), and a bundle upgrade is peipkg's.

field: object.certificate.digest            required
field: object.certificate.name              required
field: object.certificate.purposes          required
  The purposes the root is trusted for now.

--- event trustd.root.removed
tier: standard
gating: none — every root that leaves the set in force, other than by distrust, is recorded
cardinality: one per root that left the set

A root certificate authority left the set this machine trusts, and no
distrust names it: its addition was withdrawn, or a bundle upgrade dropped
it. A root that leaves because it was distrusted is
`trustd.root.distrusted` instead.

No subject, as for `trustd.root.added`.

field: object.certificate.digest            required
field: object.certificate.name              required
field: object.certificate.purposes          required
  The purposes the root was trusted for until it left.

--- event trustd.root.distrusted
tier: standard
gating: none — every root a distrust takes out of the set in force is recorded
cardinality: one per root that left the set

A distrust took a root certificate authority out of the set this machine
trusts. A distrust applies to shipped roots and to additions alike. A
distrust naming a certificate the machine does not have takes nothing out
and writes no record; if that certificate arrives later it is refused, and
never enters the set to leave it.

No subject, as for `trustd.root.added`.

field: object.certificate.digest            required
field: object.certificate.name              required
field: object.certificate.purposes          required
  The purposes the root was trusted for until it was distrusted.
